<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ponce, Puerto Rico</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ponce is the largest city on the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a> y Loayza, great-grandson of the island's first governor. It grew rich from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and trade in the 19th century. On 21 March 1937 police fired on a Nationalist Party march there, killing 19 people in the Ponce massacre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ponce-puerto-rico.jpg" alt="Ponce, Puerto Rico" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mtmelendez · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castillo_Serralles.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ponce lies on the southern coastal plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, on the shore of the Caribbean Sea, south of Adjuntas, Utuado and Jayuya and between Peñuelas and Juana Díaz. The municipality has 31 barrios, 12 of them in the urban area, and is the second largest on the island by land area. It includes Cerro de Punta, the island's highest peak at 1,340 metres, and part of the Toro Negro Forest. Its historic district, Ponce Pueblo, lies about 4.8 kilometres inland from the coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> territory of Guaynia. Agüeybaná, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> who led it, was among those who greeted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a> when he came to the island in 1508. Four archaeological sites in the municipality are significant: Canas, Tibes, Caracoles and El Bronce. The city was founded on 12 August 1692 and is named after Juan Ponce de León y Loayza, great-grandson of the conquistador. Its first mayor was Pedro Sánchez de Matos. In the early 19th century Ponce was still one of dozens of hamlets on the island, whose people lived on subsistence agriculture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raising and maritime contraband with foreigners.</p><p>During the 19th century a new architectural style, later called Ponce Creole, was born in the city, and architects mixed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a> and neoclassical styles. The Teatro La Perla was built in 1864. Ponce was the first city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> where Protestant churches were built, and the Ponce Cathedral dates from 1839. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> invaded Puerto Rico in 1898, Ponce was the largest city on the island, with a population of 22,000, and it had the best road to San Juan and underwater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> cables to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>.</p><p>The taking of the port by American troops was a turning point in the campaign, since it let them bring in men and war material. Bomba and Plena music grew up mainly in the south of the island, and Ponce, especially Barrio San Antón, is known as a birthplace of these rhythms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ponce became the first municipality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> to obtain autonomy, on 27 October 1992, under the Autonomous Municipalities Act. Its historic centre has been revitalised under the Ponce en Marcha project, and it has many museums. The economy was long based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> and now rests on manufacturing, retail and tourism, and the Port of Ponce is the island's chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> port. Ponce has 166,327 inhabitants according to the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and March 21 is observed each year in memory of the Ponce massacre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ponce, Puerto Rico” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In <b>Ponce, Puerto Rico</b>, Las Mañanitas is an annual celebration of Our Lady of Guadalupe: a pre-dawn parade and a Catholic Mass, followed by a free breakfast that the city government offers at the historic Ponce City Hall.</blockquote><a href="/songs/las-mananitas">Las Mañanitas</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nationalist Party of Pedro Albizu Campos demanded independence, and police killed protesters at <b>Ponce</b> in 1937.</blockquote><a href="/state/puerto-rico-united-states">Puerto Rico (United States)</a></li></ul></section>
